    id: 'input.key-handler', tag: 'STATIC',
    statement: 'Read in full: the keyboard interrupt handler (obj1+0x30907 to +0x309ea, ending in iret). It is installed by the code at obj1+0x22b5b to +0x22b98 with DOS function 0x25 for vector 9 (the keyboard), handler address obj1+0x30907, the old vector saved at obj2+0x1d5a4 and +0x1d58c; it is the only `in al, 0x60` of the program (Ghidra search_instructions over obj1, 80207 instructions, 2026-10-03). The handler guards against re-entry with the word obj2+0x28b30, reads the scancode from port 0x60, acknowledges the keyboard through port 0x61 (the high bit set and cleared again) and the interrupt controller (0x20 to port 0x20), replaces the prefix code 0xe0 with the previous code kept in the byte obj2+0x28cd0, and takes a code above 0x52 as a key release (down flag 0, the code minus 0x80) and any other code as a press (down flag 1). Codes 0x1c (Enter) and 0x39 (Space) write the down flag to the byte obj2+0x28cce, 0x44 (F10) to obj2+0x28ccd, 0x48 (Up) to obj2+0x28cdb and 0x50 (Down) to obj2+0x28cd2. Then for each of the 12 words of the key table (the table pointer at obj2+0x1d990, set to obj2+0x1b6d8; the six words of each of the two players, cfg.default-keys), when the low byte equals the code it writes the down flag to the word\'s high byte. It finally stores the code in obj2+0x28cd0. So the high byte of a key table word is 1 while that key is held, which is what input.buttons and input.steer-flags read. The table, the last code and the five fixed flag bytes were observed at run time (input.key-state); the extended-key prefix and the steering flags were not.',
