    id: 'static.table-meanings', tag: 'STATIC',
    statement: 'What the six start-up tables are, from their readers (resolves the earlier UNKNOWN_STATIC_TABLE_MEANING; the readers are in mapxy.use, mov.use, floor.table-use, cars.slots, terrain.sdx-index and effects.ground-dust, the loader below is read here): MAP.XY is the minimap\'s world to picture lookup (two byte tables of 0x800 entries, one for x and one for y, hud.minimap-draw uses them for the dots); GIG.MOV holds the per-call screen offsets (x and y words) of the player\'s reaction figure drawn at the right of the race screen with the frames of GIGGLES.SP (figure.rules); TRIG.DAT is a table of two dwords an angle, the 1920 direction vectors the floor and the turn move scale by a distance (the column and row steps along an angle, trig.use and floor.table-use), and NDIST a table of dwords whose first one the turn move multiplies the TRIG entry by to get the pivot of a turn (trig.use (1); the other readers of the NDIST buffer pointer, and so the use of its further entries, were not listed); CARS.SP is the eight drivers\' car frames, twelve a slot (cars.slots); WACKY.SDX is the table of 30 records of 0x12e bytes, one per surface value: its dword +0x120 is the fraction of the speed the surface takes away and its dword +0x124 the number of steering calls before a slide (terrain.sdx-index, surface.js), its flag bytes at +0 and +0x80 (a byte a track) say whether the track has the surface\'s first and second wheel picture, its dwords at +0x100 and +0x104 are the offsets of those pictures in EFFECTS.SP, and the words from +0x108 give their strides, sizes and frame counts (effects.ground-dust). The last 8 bytes of a record, the dwords +0x126 and +0x12a, are not in the file\'s meaning: the effects loader (obj1+0x1754c to +0x177a8, once a track load, for the records up to the count word obj2+0x28b7c) reads, for every record whose flag byte for the track is 1, the picture from EFFECTS.SP into the effects buffer (obj2+0x1d80c, a pointer that goes on by the picture\'s size) and stores the buffer address in the record, +0x126 for the first picture (obj1+0x17669) and +0x12a for the second (obj1+0x17754); a picture at an offset that an earlier record loaded already (a table of up to 40 offsets, +0x1d584 the archive) gets that record\'s address instead (obj1+0x175ef to +0x17601 and +0x176e0 to +0x176f6). So the rewritten tail is the pair of buffer pointers of the pictures loaded for the track, for as many records as have a flag byte of 1.',
